# upstream/81-attr-type-walker-iterative: the attribute and type walker
# and both replacers keep their place on an explicit stack, so their native
# depth does not grow with an attribute's or a type's nesting. Over a
# symbol reference nested in builtin arrays 10,000 deep, on a thread whose
# stack is 256 KiB, both walks meet every array and the reference, an
# interrupt at the reference ends the walk, and both replacers rebuild the
# arrays around the reference they replace (walker.cpp): what the
# compiler's substitution of a family's index terms and upstream's symbol
# uses do on every compilation.
# Building a program against MLIR's libraries takes longer than a step.
step_limit=180
. "$IDRIS_MLIR_ROOT/harness/testutils.sh"
mlir_check "walker and replacers: 10,000 nested arrays on a 256 KiB stack" "$here/walker.cpp" 10000
